.TH FERDIM 1 "2026-11-24" "ferdim 1.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ferdim \- dynamic, self-tuning download manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferdim
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBFerDim\fR is a segmented, self-tuning download manager for Linux. Its
engine opens up to 32 parallel connections per file and continuously
calibrates the connection count to saturate either the local line or the
remote server, whichever ceiling is reachable. Adaptive streaming media
(HLS and DASH manifests) is fetched as parallel segments, metalink
(.metalink / .meta4) documents expand into queued mirror sets with
checksums, and finished ZIP and TAR archives can be listed, selectively
extracted and repaired in place.
Downloads are crash-safe
(sparse part files with periodic metadata checkpoints and ETag/If-Range
validation), adapt gracefully to server throttling (HTTP 429/503), and
resume after restarts. Sources (the primary URL and its mirrors) are
health-scored: work is balanced by measured per-connection throughput, a
source that fails, stalls or serves a different file is demoted and the
interrupted byte range continues from another source. Checksum sidecar files
(.sha256, .md5, .sha512, .sha1) next to the URL are picked up automatically,
and verification completes before a job is reported as finished. A daemon exposes a Unix-socket JSON-RPC API plus a
token-protected localhost HTTP API; front-ends include this CLI, a web UI
(http://127.0.0.1:8378) and a GTK 4 application. A Firefox WebExtension
hands browser downloads and detected media over to the daemon.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI add " URL"
Queue a download. Options:
.B \-o " FILE"
output name,
.B \-d " DIR"
target directory,
.BI \-n " N"
connection count (1\(en32),
.BI \-H " \"Header: Value\""
extra HTTP header (repeatable),
.B \-c " COOKIE"
Cookie header,
.B \-r " REFERER"
Referer header,
.BI \-u / \-p " USER/PASS"
HTTP Basic authentication,
.BI \-\-proxy " URL"
proxy (http, https, socks5 or socks5h; a socks5h proxy resolves names),
.BI \-\-mirror " URL"
additional mirror for the same file (repeatable; primary and mirrors are
blended by measured throughput, failing sources are demoted),
.BI \-\-sha256 " HEX" " / " \-\-md5 " / " \-\-sha1 " / " \-\-sha512
verify the finished file against a digest (all given digests are computed
in one pass; an invalid value is rejected),
.BI \-\-checksum " ALGO:HEX"
any supported digest (sha256, sha512, md5, sha1); the algorithm may be
omitted when it is unambiguous from the digest length,
.BI \-\-merkle " URL|FILE"
per-chunk integrity manifest (\fB.ferdim-merkle\fR, see \fBmerkle\fR below);
only the byte ranges of chunks that fail verification are fetched again.
Accepted for HTTP(S) jobs only; a discovered sidecar manifest that does not
match is a warning, an explicit one that cannot be repaired is an error.
.BI \-\-limit " RATE"
per-job speed limit (e.g. 2MB; KB, MB and GB are 1024-based),
.B \-k
skip TLS verification,
.BI \-\-http3 " off|auto|force"
HTTP/3 (QUIC) transport (optional \fBaioquic\fR module): \fBauto\fR uses it
when the server advertised it in Alt-Svc and falls back to TCP on failure;
\fBforce\fR never falls back. Default: the \fIhttp3\fR setting (\fBoff\fR),
.B \-\-wait
follow progress until completion; returns only after verification.
.IP
A URL starting with \fB@\fR names a local text file with one URL per line
(comments start with #).
.TP
.BI hosts " [\-\-forget HOST | \-\-clear]"
Show the learned per-host connection profiles: the link count the
throughput governor settled at, the response-time band, the measured
per-link speed and the number of samples. New jobs start from the learned
link count (and from the learned safe ceiling when the host answered
429/503) instead of a cold start; the governor keeps probing in both
directions, so maximum power remains the default. \fB\-\-forget\fR drops
one host, \fB\-\-clear\fR all of them. Set \fIlearn_hosts\fR to \fBfalse\fR
to turn learning off.
.TP
.BI add-file " FILE..."
Queue every URL listed in a text file, or every file described by a local
\&.metalink / .meta4 document.
.TP
.BI list
Show the queue.
.TP
.BI pause " ID|all" \ ( resume ", " cancel ", " restart ", " remove )
Control jobs; \fBremove \-\-delete\-files\fR also discards partial data.
.TP
.BI limit " RATE"
Global speed limit (\fB0\fR disables). KB, MB and GB are 1024-based.
Setting a limit while turbo mode is on leaves turbo mode, so the applied
limit and the turbo state never disagree. A limit set by hand also wins
over a speed plan until \fBlimit plan\fR gives control back.
.TP
.BI speedplan " [show|set JSON|clear|resume]"
Time-of-day speed profile on top of the schedule windows, for example
2 MB/s during work hours and maximum power at night. Rules are a JSON
list:
\fB[{"days":"mon-fri","from":"09:00","to":"18:00","limit":"2MB"}]\fR.
\fIdays\fR holds weekday names or ranges (mon-fri, sat-sun, mon,wed; empty
or * = every day), \fIfrom\fR/\fIto\fR are HH:MM and may cross midnight,
and \fIlimit\fR is 1024-based ("0" or "" = unlimited). The first matching
rule wins; when no rule matches, the plan imposes no limit, so maximum
power needs no rule of its own. The queue applies the active rule every
half second, so a boundary is honoured without restarting jobs. A limit
set by hand (\fBlimit\fR or the web speed selector) wins over the plan
until \fBspeedplan resume\fR (or \fBlimit plan\fR) hands control back;
\fBspeedplan clear\fR removes the plan and restores the limit that was in
force before it. While a plan limit applies turbo mode is left, so the
limit and the turbo state never disagree. The web settings show the rules
as readable rows and edit them as JSON.
.TP
.BI metrics
Print the Prometheus text exposition format (0.0.4) for local monitoring:
bytes downloaded, jobs that reached a terminal state, live links, retries
and HTTP 429/503 throttles per host, aggregate throughput, current queue
states, coexistence pressure and build information. The daemon serves the
same text at \fB/metrics\fR on the localhost HTTP port and requires the
usual token header:
\fBcurl \-H "X\-FerDim\-Token: $(cat ~/.local/state/ferdim/token)"
http://127.0.0.1:8378/metrics\fR. A query-string token is not accepted;
host labels are host names only (no credentials or paths) and at most 200
hosts are exported separately, the rest summed under \fBother\fR.
.TP
.BI turbo " on|off|status"
Toggle maximum-power defaults (32 links, unlimited speed, 8 concurrent jobs).
.TP
.BI coexist " on|off|status"
Opt-in coexistence mode; off by default, so maximum power stays the default.
While it is on the line is sampled once a second (\fI/proc/net/dev\fR;
loopback and virtual interfaces are left out) and FerDim's own throughput is
subtracted, leaving the foreign traffic. When that exceeds
\fIcoexist_threshold\fR (default \fB0.15\fR, i.e. 15% of the observed line
capacity) for a few seconds, every job gives up half of its connections (at
least one stays) and the governor stops probing upwards; after ten quiet
seconds the cap is released and links are rebuilt. The footer of the web UI
shows the state.
.TP
.BI merkle " make FILE"
Write \fIFILE.ferdim-merkle\fR: content-defined (FastCDC, 256 KiB / 1 MiB /
4 MiB) chunk offsets, a SHA-256 per chunk and the Merkle root over the
chunk hashes. A publisher can host the manifest next to the file like a
\&.sha256 sidecar; a downloader picks up \fI<url>.ferdim-merkle\fR
automatically.
.TP
.BI merkle " verify FILE [MANIFEST]"
Re-hash the chunks listed in the manifest (default \fIFILE.ferdim-merkle\fR)
and report either the matching root or the byte ranges of the corrupted
chunks. Verification reads the file once; chunking is not re-run.
.TP
.BI merkle " update FILE URL \-\-manifest " MANIFEST
Build the new version of \fIURL\fR by keeping every chunk whose bytes already
exist in \fIFILE\fR (content-defined boundaries make this robust to
insertions) and fetching only the missing byte ranges with HTTP Range. The
result is verified chunk by chunk and only then moved over the target
(\fB\-o\fR picks another target).
.TP
.BI publish " DIR " \-\-base\-url " URL"
Emit the publisher side of a download site: for every regular file in
\fIDIR\fR write \fIFILE.ferdim-merkle\fR (content defined chunks and the
Merkle root, see \fBmerkle\fR above), \fIFILE.sha256\fR (coreutils format)
and \fIFILE.meta4\fR, an RFC 5854 document carrying name, size, SHA\-256,
the merkle metaurl (\fIapplication/x-ferdim-merkle\fR) and merkle root plus
the download addresses: \fIURL\fR joined with the file name has priority
1, every \fB\-\-mirror\fR follows with priority 2 and up. One
\fIindex.meta4\fR covers the whole directory, so \fBferdim add-file\fR on
either document queues the artefacts with their mirrors and digests. The
output is byte stable: files are processed in name order, the only
timestamp is \fB<published>\fR taken from the artefact mtime (RFC 3339,
UTC) and unchanged files are not rewritten, so re\-running produces no
diff. A manifest that is already current (same size, not older than the
file) is reused; \fB\-\-force\fR regenerates it anyway, \fB\-\-no-merkle\fR
skips the chunk manifest and drops its metaurl, and \fB\-\-glob\fR limits
the set to matching file names. Names are percent\-encoded in URLs, sub
directories are not descended into.
.TP
.BI config " [\-\-set JSON]"
Show or change settings: segments, concurrency, scheduler window and
per-category windows (\fIschedule.categories\fR), language, watched
folders (\fIwatch_dirs\fR, \fIwatch_done\fR = delete | rename | keep),
automatic sidecar verification (\fIchecksum_sidecar\fR), desktop
notifications (\fInotify\fR, default true; \fInotify_errors_only\fR
reports failures only), retries and
timeouts (\fItimeout\fR, \fIstall_timeout\fR), coexistence mode
(\fIcoexist\fR, \fIcoexist_threshold\fR), the time-of-day speed plan
(\fIspeed_plan\fR, \fIspeed_plan_manual\fR) and download history
retention (\fIhistory_days\fR, default 365 days, \fB0\fR keeps forever).
.TP
.BI history " [QUERY]"
Show the persistent job history (SQLite database in the state directory),
newest first. \fB\-\-state\fR filters by \fBdone\fR, \fBerror\fR or
\fBcanceled\fR; \fB\-\-limit\fR caps the result. \fB\-\-stats\fR prints the
total bytes served, the number of records and per-host averages (links and
speed). \fB\-\-redownload\fR \fIID\fR queues the saved URL and options
again. \fB\-\-clear\fR removes everything, or with \fB\-\-older\-than\fR
\fIDAYS\fR only records older than that. A password embedded in a URL is
kept out of the stored address; it is retained only for re-download.
.TP
.BI archive " list|extract|repair" " ID"
Work on a finished archive: ZIP (nested inner ZIP members included) and
TAR, .tar.gz, .tar.xz and .tar.bz2. \fBlist\fR prints the entries;
\fB\-\-nested\fR also lists the members of inner ZIP archives, shown as
\fIouter.zip!/inner.zip!/file\fR. \fBextract\fR writes the given members
(all when none are named) into the target directory: by default a
directory named after the archive, next to it; \fB\-\-dest\fR takes an
absolute path. Extraction is path-safe: entries with \fB..\fR, absolute
paths or escaping links are refused, and an existing file that this tool
did not write is kept unless \fB\-\-force\fR is given. Each member is
written to \fIname.part\fR and renamed into place, and the listing cap
does not limit extraction. \fBrepair\fR verifies the recorded
\fI.ferdim-extract.json\fR manifest (size and CRC32) and re-extracts only
missing or damaged members, so an interrupted extraction resumes where it
stopped. Options: \fB\-\-nested\fR (list), \fB\-\-dest\fR \fIDIR\fR and
\fB\-\-force\fR (extract, repair).
.TP
.BI daemon
Run the RPC/HTTP daemon in the foreground (normally started via the
\fBferdim.service\fR systemd user unit).
.TP
.BI web
Open the web UI in the default browser.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/ferdim/
Socket, token, PID, queue state, job metadata and the download history
database (\fIhistory.db\fR, mode 0600); learned host profiles live in
\fIhosts.json\fR (same mode, at most 500 hosts, entries older than 90
days are dropped).
.TP
.I ~/.config/ferdim/config.json
User configuration (see \fBferdim config\fR; invalid values are reset to
their defaults at start).
.TP
Files dropped into a watched folder are queued automatically: .metalink /
\&.meta4 documents and .txt / .urls / .list text files with one URL per line.
By default the file is deleted after it is queued.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ferdim/ferdim-extension.zip
Unsigned Firefox extension bundle; sign once with
.BR web-ext (1)
or load temporarily via about:debugging.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation: https://github.com/proxy-turkey/ferdim
